    "title": "The Bridge from Its Own Doorstep",
    "headlinePhrase": "Bridge",
    "dek": "Looking along the Golden Gate from the San Francisco end, steel arch, brick fort and all.",
    "paragraphs": [
      "From the beaches, the Golden Gate is a postcard. From its own southern end, it's a machine.",
      "This view runs along the bridge from the city side. On the left, the approach structure dominates: heavy concrete piers and a red steel framework of trusses and girders, with the roadway riding on top and streetlights standing along it. In the middle, a great steel arch spans the gap below the road. Beyond it, the deck reaches out over the water to the south tower, which rises tall and red near the right of the frame, its main cables sweeping up from the left and down again toward the north tower in the distance.",
      "Near the bottom of the frame, just beyond a band of dark green trees, the brick walls of Fort Point peek out below the deck. That relationship, an old brick fort tucked beneath a steel span, was a big part of why I chose this angle.",
      "The water below the bridge is a calm, milky turquoise. The tower's shadow falls long across it, a dark diagonal running toward the camera. Across the strait, the hills of the Marin side are brown and gold, dotted with dark trees. The sky is a deep, saturated blue, almost navy at the top, fading paler toward the horizon on the right.",
      "I placed the south tower roughly on the right third and let the approach structure fill the left edge. That gives the picture weight on the left and height on the right, and the cables connect them. It's a composition about engineering more than romance: concrete, steel, rivets, repeated shapes.",
      "The colour of the bridge in clear light is hard to describe. Officially orange, it reads deep red against a blue sky, and almost rust where it's in shadow. Here the sun was strong enough to give the steel its brightest red on the tower and the cables, while the trusses at left fall into darker tones."
    ],
    "settings": "Sony A7R III · Sony FE 16-35mm F2.8 GM II · 32mm · 1/20s · f/9 · ISO 100",
    "note": "32mm, near the long end of the 16-35mm, keeps the approach structure from distorting while still fitting the tower and the far shore into the frame. f/9 gives depth of field from the trees at the bottom to the north tower in the distance. 1/20s is a slower shutter than a bright scene like this would normally give at f/9 and ISO 100. At 1/20s, a steady support or very careful handholding is needed.",
