    "expectSrc": "/images/gallery/lightbox/lukasz-jagiello-19-full.webp",
    "title": "Emerald Water, Pink Sky",
    "headlinePhrase": "Water",
    "dek": "Along Highway 199, the Smith River runs green and clear through a canyon of grey rock and dark forest.",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Some rivers are clear. The Smith is something else: green, transparent, and so still in places that it reflects the sky like a mirror.",
      "In this frame, the river enters from the middle distance and curves toward the bottom right corner. Upstream, it's a ribbon of pale water running over stones between banks of grey rock. As it comes closer, it slows and deepens into a broad pool, emerald green and glassy, the reflections of the sky and trees laid across its surface. At the lower right, the water is clear enough to show the rounded stones on the riverbed, a few white boulders breaking the surface near the bank.",
      "The banks are pale grey rock, layered and angular, the kind of stone that looks freshly broken even though the river has been working on it for a very long time. On the right, the rock rises into a steep bank thick with conifers and shrubs. On the left, more forest, a few tall, scraggly trees standing above the rest against the sky. The trees climb up both sides of the canyon and fade into hazy blue ridges in the distance.",
      "And above all of it, the sky: soft grey-blue with streaks of pink cloud drifting across it. It's not a blazing sunset. It's a gentler colour, a pink glow on the undersides of a few clouds, and it turns the river's reflections faintly warm too.",
      "I chose a vertical composition so the river could lead from the bottom of the frame deep into the canyon. The foreground is a tangle of green shrubs and dry grass on the near bank, a little rough and messy, which I like. It gives you somewhere to stand.",
      "What I wanted to capture was the colour of the water. It's hard to believe until you see it in person. That green comes from the clarity of the river and the stones beneath, and in the soft light of evening, it was at its richest.",
      "Some landscapes shout. This one hums."
    ],
    "settings": "Sony A7R III · Sony FE 16-35mm F2.8 GM II · 35mm · 1/4s · f/9 · ISO 100",
    "note": "At 35mm, the long end of the 16-35mm, I could frame the river's curve and the canyon without making the distant ridges look tiny. 1/4s smooths the moving water slightly and softens the reflections, while f/9 keeps everything from the near bank to the far trees in focus. At ISO 100, the deep greens and the soft pink sky stay clean. A quarter of a second needs a steady support, especially with fine detail in the trees.",
    "date": "2026-10-09"
